00:01:56.783 --> 00:02:01.020
OK, but we still got to get
through the rest of acids

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:02.689
and bases.

00:02:02.689 --> 00:02:06.626
Now this is where we left off.

00:02:06.626 --> 00:02:09.929
This way of thinking
about acids and bases

00:02:09.929 --> 00:02:12.565
is where we left off on
Wednesday, all right?

00:02:13.433 --> 00:02:17.003
And this is the
Arrhenius-- oh, Svante.

00:02:17.003 --> 00:02:19.873
Oh, Svante-- he was
the first-- remember,

00:02:19.873 --> 00:02:21.808
[INAUDIBLE] over thousands
of years would say,

00:02:21.808 --> 00:02:23.276
this is bitter.

00:02:23.276 --> 00:02:24.310
Put it in that category.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:25.311
This is sour.

00:02:25.311 --> 00:02:27.079
Mmm, right?

00:02:27.079 --> 00:02:28.581
Acid-- no, base.

00:02:28.581 --> 00:02:29.082
Acid.

00:02:29.082 --> 00:02:32.519
And there are ways that people
categorized acids and bases

00:02:32.519 --> 00:02:34.320
for a long time.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:37.490
But nobody really started
to try to understand them

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:40.994
at the atomic scale until Svante
came along and said, look,

00:02:40.994 --> 00:02:45.732
it's all about the H+
and the OH-, right?

00:02:45.732 --> 00:02:48.768
So acids-- and this is what
we talked about on Wednesday.

00:02:48.768 --> 00:02:54.507
Acids are donating H+ ions,
protons, into solution,

00:02:54.507 --> 00:02:55.308
into water.

00:02:55.308 --> 00:02:59.245
And bases are donating OH-,
adding more of those types

00:02:59.245 --> 00:03:00.547
of ions into the water.

00:03:00.547 --> 00:03:05.852
And that's what makes them have
the properties they have, OK?

00:03:05.852 --> 00:03:09.088
Now, so if you have--

00:03:09.088 --> 00:03:10.023
so let's see.

00:03:10.023 --> 00:03:13.226
So H-- I'm going to just
write this one on the board.

00:03:13.226 --> 00:03:15.495
Because we're going to talk
about these two reactions--

00:03:15.495 --> 00:03:17.630
plus H2O--

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:23.303
and remember, the water is
just a kind of spectator there.

00:03:23.303 --> 00:03:24.003
It's a liquid.

00:03:24.003 --> 00:03:24.837
It's everywhere.

00:03:24.837 --> 00:03:26.439
It's the thing
you're dissolving in.

00:03:26.439 --> 00:03:27.941
It's the solvent.

00:03:27.941 --> 00:03:29.442
That's the water.

00:03:29.442 --> 00:03:32.712
So you don't include
it in these--

00:03:32.712 --> 00:03:35.180
if it's just the liquid that
you're dissolving something,

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:38.384
it's not what you include
in things like equilibrium

00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:41.487
constant expressions.

00:03:41.487 --> 00:03:43.122
OK, so that's going to be--

00:03:43.122 --> 00:03:45.525
oh, but these guys, these
are dissolved in the liquid,

00:03:45.525 --> 00:03:46.025
all right?

00:03:46.025 --> 00:03:47.427
These are the ions--

00:03:47.427 --> 00:03:48.228
plus CL-.

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:57.637
OK, now in this
particular case, you

00:03:57.637 --> 00:04:01.074
can write the equilibrium
constant, right?

00:04:01.074 --> 00:04:02.542
And that's the acid.

00:04:02.542 --> 00:04:05.545
It's also [INAUDIBLE] the
acid dissociation constant.

00:04:05.545 --> 00:04:08.281
Remember, we had the
solubility product constant.

00:04:08.281 --> 00:04:10.950
And we also can
write the Ka, right?

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:14.053
So Ka for this would be--

00:04:14.053 --> 00:04:18.992
following our normal K
equilibrium constant procedure,

00:04:18.992 --> 00:04:21.894
it would look something
like this, right?

00:04:21.894 --> 00:04:25.298
Now here's the thing--

00:04:25.298 --> 00:04:30.236
for this particular
reaction of--

00:04:30.236 --> 00:04:34.907
for this particular dissociation
of HCl, of this acid--

00:04:34.907 --> 00:04:37.409
and remember, it's an acid
because it's giving us H+.

00:04:37.409 --> 00:04:40.713
Oh, H+ doesn't stay H+.

00:04:40.713 --> 00:04:42.915
Remember, that's also what
we talked about Wednesday.

00:04:42.915 --> 00:04:46.119
H+ is actually H3O+.

00:04:46.119 --> 00:04:48.921
But the H+ is not
stable in pure water.

00:04:48.921 --> 00:04:51.724
But sometimes-- actually,
very often-- you'll see it--

00:04:51.724 --> 00:04:54.927
in textbooks as well-- you'll
see it just written as H+.

00:04:54.927 --> 00:04:57.697
But we know that
it's the same thing.

00:04:57.697 --> 00:05:02.035
H3O-- if it's in water, it's
H3O+, right, OK, so don't let--

00:05:02.035 --> 00:05:04.737
but this is something
like 10 to the sixth.

00:05:04.737 --> 00:05:07.540
That's huge.

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:11.010
That equilibrium is so far over.

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:16.082
That equilibrium is so far over
given this enormous equilibrium

00:05:16.082 --> 00:05:20.286
constant, that we often will
say that this reaction has

00:05:20.286 --> 00:05:21.487
gone to completion.

00:05:25.224 --> 00:05:28.027
Now we know that
it's not infinite.

00:05:28.027 --> 00:05:31.331
So it hasn't-- technically
there's still a little arrow

00:05:31.331 --> 00:05:34.367
going that way, all right?

00:05:34.367 --> 00:05:41.140
But-- and so, but since it's
so much almost to completion,

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:44.043
you'll often also see it written
with just one arrow, all right?

00:05:44.043 --> 00:05:45.345
So I wanted to make that clear.

00:05:45.345 --> 00:05:47.447
But we know that
it's an equilibrium.

00:05:47.447 --> 00:05:48.981
This has found an equilibrium.

00:05:48.981 --> 00:05:51.351
So there's still a little
bit happening the other way,

00:05:51.351 --> 00:05:53.386
but just so little, all right?

00:05:53.386 --> 00:05:56.956
So you'll often see that
going to completion.

00:05:56.956 --> 00:06:02.228
OK, good-- getting us
back into the mood.

00:06:02.228 --> 00:06:09.168
Now for the base,
we can have NaOH--

00:06:09.168 --> 00:06:10.403
let's do that one--

00:06:10.403 --> 00:06:20.113
plus H2O goes to Na+
in solution plus OH-.

00:06:20.113 --> 00:06:22.548
And this is the Arrhenius
acid-base definition--

00:06:22.548 --> 00:06:23.816
gesundheit.

00:06:23.816 --> 00:06:30.690
And you could write a similar
expression for the base, Na+,

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:33.058
OH-.

00:06:33.058 --> 00:06:35.261
And that's going
to go over NaOH.

00:06:35.261 --> 00:06:42.835
Now this is also very large.

00:06:42.835 --> 00:06:44.871
Now these are both strong.

00:06:44.871 --> 00:06:47.507
And we're going to come back to
this at the end of the lecture.

00:06:47.507 --> 00:06:49.742
I want to talk about what
makes an acid or a base

00:06:49.742 --> 00:06:51.344
strong or weak.

00:06:51.344 --> 00:06:52.945
These are both strong.

00:06:52.945 --> 00:06:55.415
And just as a little preview
to where we're getting,

00:06:55.415 --> 00:06:57.683
that's because they
dissociate so much.

00:06:57.683 --> 00:07:01.220
So this also has
a very high Kb--

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:03.523
Kb, because we're
talking about a base--

00:07:03.523 --> 00:07:06.192
Kb, Ka, Kb, right?

00:07:06.192 --> 00:07:09.395
Oh, but some people don't
like these large exponents,

00:07:09.395 --> 00:07:13.299
these large or small 10 to the
minus 5th, 10 to the positive

00:07:13.299 --> 00:07:14.033
fifth.

00:07:14.033 --> 00:07:16.536
And so we take the log.

00:07:16.536 --> 00:07:18.371
You can take the log of these.

00:07:18.371 --> 00:07:24.544
And then you'll get, like,
a pKb, right, or pKb,

00:07:24.544 --> 00:07:26.946
which is the power of that.

00:07:26.946 --> 00:07:34.954
So log of Kb would be
low, right, would be low.

00:07:34.954 --> 00:07:36.355
If this is going
to be high, then

00:07:36.355 --> 00:07:38.591
minus the log of the power
of that is going to be low.

00:07:38.591 --> 00:07:44.397
So you can talk about
pKb, Kb, Ka, pKa.

00:07:44.397 --> 00:07:47.667
It's all just thinking about
these equilibrium constants.

00:07:47.667 --> 00:07:51.671
And we'll come back
to this in a second.

00:07:51.671 --> 00:07:53.606
If I had a question for you--

00:07:53.606 --> 00:07:57.643
if I said, OK, how
would you use this--

00:07:57.643 --> 00:08:01.280
if I said that I've got point--

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:02.849
I'm going to give
you a problem now--

00:08:02.849 --> 00:08:12.258
0.05 moles per liter of NaOH,
and I asked you, what's the pH,

00:08:12.258 --> 00:08:14.760
well, you can do
it now, all right?

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:15.294
Why?

00:08:15.294 --> 00:08:20.500
Because I've just told you
it's a almost fully dissociated

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:21.501
base.

00:08:21.501 --> 00:08:25.571
So the equilibrium constant for
that dissociation of that base

00:08:25.571 --> 00:08:26.606
is very, very high.

00:08:26.606 --> 00:08:29.675
It means it lies very
far over to the right.

00:08:29.675 --> 00:08:32.010
So basically, what
does that mean?

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:34.780
If I've got 0.05 moles
per liter starting out,

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:35.780
it's mostly dissociated.

00:08:35.780 --> 00:08:36.782
[SNEEZE]

00:08:36.782 --> 00:08:38.284
Gesundheit.

00:08:38.284 --> 00:08:44.991
So I've got 0.05 moles per
liter of Na+ and OH-, all right?

00:08:44.991 --> 00:08:47.693
OK, but the OH- is what matters.

00:08:47.693 --> 00:08:53.866
So full dissociation-- oh, we're
going to do this because I want

00:08:53.866 --> 00:08:55.835
to be precise here--

00:08:55.835 --> 00:08:57.169
put that in quotes.

00:08:57.169 --> 00:09:02.775
It's almost full-- means that
I have 0.05 moles per liter

00:09:02.775 --> 00:09:04.510
of OH---

00:09:04.510 --> 00:09:06.312
by the way, and Na+.

00:09:06.312 --> 00:09:09.048
But Na+ is a spectator
in terms of the pH.

00:09:09.048 --> 00:09:12.084
It's not going to change
the pH, right, the Na+?

00:09:12.084 --> 00:09:14.453
Oh, but the OH will, all right?

00:09:14.453 --> 00:09:17.690
And so now we go
back to these Ks.

00:09:17.690 --> 00:09:21.460
We know-- right, we know--

00:09:21.460 --> 00:09:24.263
that the equilibrium constant
of pure water is equal

00:09:24.263 --> 00:09:31.971
to the concentration of H+
in solution, which is H3O+,

00:09:31.971 --> 00:09:36.108
times OH-, and that that's
equal to 10 to the minus 14th,

00:09:36.108 --> 00:09:36.609
all right?

00:09:36.609 --> 00:09:42.048
That's another thing that we
talked about on Wednesday.

00:09:42.048 --> 00:09:44.150
So if that's true, if the--

00:09:44.150 --> 00:09:48.120
which it is at 25 C-- the
equilibrium constant for water,

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:48.788
which is--

00:09:48.788 --> 00:09:51.090
remember, water
can be either one.

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:52.290
It's this times this.

00:09:52.290 --> 00:09:53.826
And we know what this is.

00:09:53.826 --> 00:09:56.862
Now you know what
that is, all right?

00:09:56.862 --> 00:09:58.397
And if you know what H+--

00:09:58.397 --> 00:10:02.134
if the concentration of
H+ is known, then the pH--

00:10:02.134 --> 00:10:04.904
power of-- the p is
the minus lower--

00:10:04.904 --> 00:10:08.341
the pH of that is something
you can calculate.

00:10:08.341 --> 00:10:16.515
And so the pH winds up,
here, being 12.7, all rigth?

00:10:16.515 --> 00:10:19.952
And that's the kind of thing
that you can have fun with.

00:10:19.952 --> 00:10:25.691
You dissolve some base, and
you can measure a pH, or a pOH,

00:10:25.691 --> 00:10:26.759
if you wanted--

00:10:26.759 --> 00:10:30.262
all stuff we talked
about last week.

00:10:30.262 --> 00:10:31.764
OK, but now we move on.

00:10:31.764 --> 00:10:36.936
And now we're going to introduce
the next definition of acids

00:10:36.936 --> 00:10:40.506
and bases by first talking about
what happens when you mix them

00:10:40.506 --> 00:10:42.341
together, all right?

00:10:42.341 --> 00:10:44.710
These are not
mixing together yet.

00:10:44.710 --> 00:10:47.313
They're just-- a base
dissolves in water.

00:10:47.313 --> 00:10:49.649
An acid dissolves in water.

00:10:49.649 --> 00:10:55.087
But what if I had an acid and a
base, and I mix them together.

00:10:55.087 --> 00:11:04.764
OK, well, so if I did that,
then I would have HCl plus NaOH.

00:11:04.764 --> 00:11:06.999
Now I'm just going to--

00:11:06.999 --> 00:11:08.434
I'm not going to
include the water.

00:11:08.434 --> 00:11:11.103
It's there on the left, and it's
going to be there on the right.

00:11:11.103 --> 00:11:13.339
I'm just going to write all
these ions down that happen,

00:11:13.339 --> 00:11:13.606
right?

00:11:13.606 --> 00:11:14.707
So you're going to get--

00:11:14.707 --> 00:11:16.342
oh, you're going to get H+--

00:11:16.342 --> 00:11:19.445
oh, we know it's H3O+--

00:11:19.445 --> 00:11:26.786
plus Cl- plus Na+.

00:11:26.786 --> 00:11:29.121
OK, these are all
dissolved in water.

00:11:29.121 --> 00:11:33.726
I'm emphasizing that with the
little aqueous subscripts.

00:11:33.726 --> 00:11:35.828
And you can see what's
going to happen now, right?

00:11:35.828 --> 00:11:37.897
So if I have these there,
well, look at this.

00:11:37.897 --> 00:11:41.233
The Na+ and the Cl-, boy, do--

00:11:41.233 --> 00:11:43.069
they see each other,
and they're like, well,

00:11:43.069 --> 00:11:47.940
maybe we could form some salt.
Maybe we could form some salt.

00:11:47.940 --> 00:11:51.177
And then the OH and the
H, and those form water.

00:11:51.177 --> 00:11:56.348
And so you can
get H2O plus NaCl.

00:11:56.348 --> 00:11:59.518
And so what happens is
what's written there,

00:11:59.518 --> 00:12:04.657
is that when you mix an acid
with a base, you will get water

00:12:04.657 --> 00:12:06.092
and some salt.
Now remember, salt

00:12:06.092 --> 00:12:12.398
is a general term, all right,
for these cations and anions,

00:12:12.398 --> 00:12:13.999
right, coming
together in a solid.

00:12:13.999 --> 00:12:18.070
So OK, now here's the
thing though, all right?

00:12:18.070 --> 00:12:21.507
So this was the acid and
I'm writing this explicitly.

00:12:21.507 --> 00:12:24.243
Because you'll see--
and this was the base.

00:12:26.912 --> 00:12:28.848
Because what is neutralization?

00:12:28.848 --> 00:12:32.451
Well, you think about
it in terms of pH.

00:12:32.451 --> 00:12:35.888
I had something with maybe a
low pH, or maybe a high pH,

00:12:35.888 --> 00:12:39.158
and I want it to become neutral.

00:12:39.158 --> 00:12:42.061
Neutral would be--
if I'm in pure water,

00:12:42.061 --> 00:12:44.163
Kw is 10 to the minus 14th.

00:12:44.163 --> 00:12:48.200
Neutral would be-- it's the
same acid and base ratio.

00:12:48.200 --> 00:12:51.804
So these are each 10 to
the minus seventh, pH 7,

00:12:51.804 --> 00:12:53.539
neutral, all right?

00:12:53.539 --> 00:12:58.544
There's no excess of
protons or OH-, all right?

00:12:58.544 --> 00:13:00.279
So if there's no
excess, it's neutral.

00:13:00.279 --> 00:13:05.451
So neutralization is basically
the act of making it neutral.

00:13:05.451 --> 00:13:07.486
So I had something
that gave a lot of H+.

00:13:07.486 --> 00:13:11.223
And now I got something
that gives me a lot of OH-.

00:13:11.223 --> 00:13:15.060
If I have the same amount
of them, all right--

00:13:15.060 --> 00:13:18.631
so they're equivalent-- then
you can make it neutral.

00:13:18.631 --> 00:13:21.801
That's neutralization, OK?

00:13:21.801 --> 00:13:24.804
But see, the thing
is I can take HCl,

00:13:24.804 --> 00:13:29.041
and I can mix Windex,
or ammonia, with it

00:13:29.041 --> 00:13:32.278
and neutralize it.

00:13:32.278 --> 00:13:34.046
And back then, they
were like, but that

00:13:34.046 --> 00:13:36.081
doesn't make any sense.

00:13:36.081 --> 00:13:39.618
But first of all, because
we don't have Windex yet.

00:13:39.618 --> 00:13:44.723
But second of all, because
Windex doesn't have any OH-.

00:13:44.723 --> 00:13:46.425
Gesundheit.

00:13:46.425 --> 00:13:49.261
And so that got them
really thinking, all right?

00:13:49.261 --> 00:13:50.930
So if I take-- so
let's take NH3.

00:13:54.033 --> 00:13:55.401
So if I take NH3--

00:13:55.401 --> 00:13:57.036
dah.

00:13:57.036 --> 00:14:04.143
If I take NH3 and I go
like this, NH3 plus HCl--

00:14:04.143 --> 00:14:05.344
now this is not--

00:14:05.344 --> 00:14:09.448
I'm not writing the liquid
dissociation of the thing.

00:14:09.448 --> 00:14:12.451
I'm mixing it, acid
and something else

00:14:12.451 --> 00:14:13.686
that I know neutralizes it.

00:14:13.686 --> 00:14:15.421
And in fact, what
ends up happening

00:14:15.421 --> 00:14:17.423
is you get-- gesundheit.

00:14:17.423 --> 00:14:18.958
You get this.

00:14:22.127 --> 00:14:29.401
And so what we know is
that this is neutralizing.

00:14:29.401 --> 00:14:33.305
It's taking-- what's
happening in this reaction?

00:14:33.305 --> 00:14:36.141
What's actually happening?

00:14:36.141 --> 00:14:37.710
What did the--
what did that base

00:14:37.710 --> 00:14:42.548
do effectively to
neutralize HCl?

00:14:42.548 --> 00:14:47.286
What it did is it took
H+ from the solution.

00:14:47.286 --> 00:14:49.889
That's what it did, all right?

00:14:49.889 --> 00:14:56.295
So NH3-- in this
reaction, NH3 takes.

00:14:56.295 --> 00:15:01.267
It takes H+ from the solution.

00:15:03.936 --> 00:15:07.973
That's exactly-- HCl was like,
I want to make it acidic--

00:15:07.973 --> 00:15:09.708
H+, H+, H+.

00:15:09.708 --> 00:15:12.578
And NH3 is like,
no, give me the H+.

00:15:12.578 --> 00:15:13.445
You cannot make this.

00:15:13.445 --> 00:15:15.414
I'm neutralizing you.

00:15:15.414 --> 00:15:18.284
I'm taking your H+ back.

00:15:18.284 --> 00:15:20.419
If it takes that, then
that's neutralizing.

00:15:20.419 --> 00:15:28.694
That's going to neutralize
the HCl as well.

00:15:28.694 --> 00:15:29.828
But see, we have a problem.

00:15:29.828 --> 00:15:36.435
Because this is not an
OH-donating molecule.

00:15:36.435 --> 00:15:37.703
But it still neutralizes.

00:15:37.703 --> 00:15:39.932
And so we need a broader
definition.

00:15:39.939 --> 00:15:40.973
And that came from--

00:15:40.973 --> 00:15:43.008
that came from
Bronsted and Lowry.

00:15:43.008 --> 00:15:46.512
And so this is another
way of thinking,

00:15:46.512 --> 00:15:50.249
a more general way of
thinking of acids and bases--

00:15:53.385 --> 00:15:56.989
more general and more
correct, because the Arrhenius

00:15:56.989 --> 00:16:01.994
definition does not cover
classes of molecules

00:16:01.994 --> 00:16:05.798
that could be a base.

00:16:05.798 --> 00:16:10.102
And so in this way, in the
Bronsted-Lowry definition--

00:16:10.102 --> 00:16:12.671
in the Bronsted-Lowry
definition--

00:16:12.671 --> 00:16:15.040
it's all about the proton.

00:16:15.040 --> 00:16:17.509
So let me write that down here.

00:16:17.509 --> 00:16:28.420
So in Bronstein and Lowry,
their definition is that acid--

00:16:28.420 --> 00:16:32.891
and he did it by looking
at acid-base neutralization

00:16:32.891 --> 00:16:34.526
reactions like I just described.

00:16:34.526 --> 00:16:39.164
Acid-base neutralization--
I'm not going to write it,

00:16:39.164 --> 00:16:40.232
because there's no room--

00:16:40.232 --> 00:16:46.672
reactions is all about
the proton transfer.

00:16:50.209 --> 00:16:54.880
These are proton
transfer reactions.

00:16:54.880 --> 00:16:58.717
This is how they saw acids and
bases, as exchanging a proton.

00:16:58.717 --> 00:17:03.522
Taking it or giving it, that's
what it's about, all right?

00:17:03.522 --> 00:17:06.458
And so in our sort of general--

00:17:06.458 --> 00:17:10.929
you know, in the general HA
lingo that we introduced,

00:17:10.929 --> 00:17:21.339
you would have HA plus B. And
that's going to go to BH+ plus

00:17:21.339 --> 00:17:21.839
A-.

00:17:24.843 --> 00:17:28.847
That's an acid-base reaction.

00:17:28.847 --> 00:17:34.286
And so you can see that these
are-- this is a proton donor.

00:17:34.286 --> 00:17:36.955
This is a proton donor.

00:17:36.955 --> 00:17:39.191
And this is an acceptor.

00:17:42.261 --> 00:17:47.499
It's a donor, H+
donor, H+ acceptor.

00:17:47.499 --> 00:17:49.935
That defines them, according
to Bronsted and Lowry,

00:17:49.935 --> 00:17:53.138
as acids and bases.

00:17:53.138 --> 00:17:55.340
OK.

00:17:55.340 --> 00:17:57.142
OK, well we can also--

00:17:57.142 --> 00:17:58.377
OK, so now let's see.

00:17:58.377 --> 00:18:00.145
Let's now go back.

00:18:00.145 --> 00:18:03.549
That's a broader definition.

00:18:03.549 --> 00:18:07.453
Let's now go back
to the ammonia, OK?

00:18:07.453 --> 00:18:09.988
And what I want to do now
is, instead of mixing it

00:18:09.988 --> 00:18:13.559
with an acid to think about
whether it trades a proton,

00:18:13.559 --> 00:18:15.694
I want to just
dissolve it in water.

00:18:15.694 --> 00:18:18.630
So now we know it's a base, OK?

00:18:18.630 --> 00:18:19.364
So what happens?

00:18:19.364 --> 00:18:25.971
So if I dissolve NH3 in H2O--

00:18:25.971 --> 00:18:29.374
I want to point something
out here, that if I do this,

00:18:29.374 --> 00:18:37.116
and I get NH4+ plus
OH-, that's the--

00:18:37.116 --> 00:18:39.051
now I'm taking a base.

00:18:39.051 --> 00:18:40.486
So there we had--

00:18:40.486 --> 00:18:41.520
there's the acid.

00:18:41.520 --> 00:18:43.021
Here's the base.

00:18:43.021 --> 00:18:44.389
That's the Arrhenius base.

00:18:44.389 --> 00:18:49.261
But now I'm taking a
Bronsted-Lowry base, right?

00:18:49.261 --> 00:18:51.296
It took an H+.

00:18:51.296 --> 00:18:56.068
Well, that means
that this is a base.

00:18:56.068 --> 00:19:02.574
But look, this-- OK,
this gave a proton.

00:19:02.574 --> 00:19:07.146
So this must be an acid
according to Bronsted--

00:19:07.146 --> 00:19:09.248
according to Bronsted and Lowry.

00:19:09.248 --> 00:19:12.851
And similarly,
over here, we know

00:19:12.851 --> 00:19:14.820
that this is going to give one.

00:19:14.820 --> 00:19:16.522
And I'm going to write
that in a second.

00:19:16.522 --> 00:19:18.690
And this would
love to accept one.

00:19:18.690 --> 00:19:20.159
So that's a base, right?

00:19:20.159 --> 00:19:21.760
And remember, we
have a name for this.

00:19:21.760 --> 00:19:24.563
In the same reaction,
we have a name for this.

00:19:24.563 --> 00:19:30.035
This would be the
conjugate, all right?

00:19:32.971 --> 00:19:34.072
Those are conjugates.

00:19:34.072 --> 00:19:41.013
That's why I'm writing
those, all right?

00:19:41.013 --> 00:19:46.185
So there's a conjugate
base-acid, conjugate acid-base.

00:19:46.185 --> 00:19:49.221
Now we have a
Bronsted-Lowry understanding

00:19:49.221 --> 00:19:51.723
of what conjugate
acid-base means.

00:19:51.723 --> 00:19:55.294
It's just a proton
transfer, all right?

00:19:55.294 --> 00:19:57.996
It's just a proton transfer.

00:19:57.996 --> 00:20:00.933
Now notice I could take this--
so I've taken this base,

00:20:00.933 --> 00:20:06.638
and I've put it into
a solution of water.

00:20:06.638 --> 00:20:09.441
I could take this acid
and do the same thing.

00:20:09.441 --> 00:20:13.545
So if I do that, I've got NH4+.

00:20:13.545 --> 00:20:16.748
And I'm going to put
that in water now, OK?

00:20:16.748 --> 00:20:18.750
So I've taken this out.

00:20:18.750 --> 00:20:21.220
And now I'm putting
that acid into water.

00:20:21.220 --> 00:20:23.689
And if I do that,
look at what happens.

00:20:23.689 --> 00:20:28.627
I get NH3 plus H3O+.

00:20:31.964 --> 00:20:34.199
Notice what happened.

00:20:34.199 --> 00:20:37.836
Well, I've switched acid.

00:20:37.836 --> 00:20:39.071
That's my acid.

00:20:39.071 --> 00:20:44.710
But this took the proton,
so it must be a base, right?

00:20:44.710 --> 00:20:47.145
And over here I've got the base.

00:20:47.145 --> 00:20:49.781
And this must be an acid.

00:20:49.781 --> 00:20:53.986
Proton transfer, that's
what's happening here--

00:20:53.986 --> 00:20:56.455
conjugates.

00:20:56.455 --> 00:20:59.992
I took the base,
and I got an acid.

00:20:59.992 --> 00:21:03.195
So this tells us that water--

00:21:03.195 --> 00:21:05.163
and it tells us what
we said on Wednesday.

00:21:05.163 --> 00:21:07.299
Water can be both.

00:21:07.299 --> 00:21:09.601
Notice water is an
acid in the one hand

00:21:09.601 --> 00:21:10.902
and a base on the other.

00:21:10.902 --> 00:21:12.137
That makes it amphoteric.

00:21:14.673 --> 00:21:20.212
So H2O can be both.

00:21:20.212 --> 00:21:22.147
It can take or give protons.

00:21:22.147 --> 00:21:23.782
And so it is amphoteric.

00:21:28.787 --> 00:21:32.291
Just for fun, if you take
the conjugate-- you know,

00:21:32.291 --> 00:21:35.494
if you take any acid-base
pair, and they're conjugates,

00:21:35.494 --> 00:21:39.331
you can also write out the
Ka's and Kb's, all right?

00:21:39.331 --> 00:21:49.941
And so if I take a
conjugate acid and base,

00:21:49.941 --> 00:21:54.279
well, you know that you
could write Ka, all right?

00:21:54.279 --> 00:21:56.548
OK, so let's write the
acid dissociation again.

00:21:56.548 --> 00:21:58.517
So for that one, here it is.

00:21:58.517 --> 00:22:03.555
Ka is NH-- is H3O+.

00:22:03.555 --> 00:22:09.328
OK, I'm going to take the bottom
one, H3O+, times NH3, right,

00:22:09.328 --> 00:22:12.431
concentrations, divided by NH4+.

00:22:16.301 --> 00:22:18.303
But if I took the
base dissociation

00:22:18.303 --> 00:22:20.372
constant from the conjugate--

00:22:20.372 --> 00:22:22.574
I'm taking the
conjugates now, right--

00:22:22.574 --> 00:22:24.910
then I would get that it's--

00:22:24.910 --> 00:22:34.986
OK, it's OH- and
NH4+ divided by NH3.

00:22:38.523 --> 00:22:40.859
Like I'm saying, just for
fun, you can now look at this.

00:22:40.859 --> 00:22:44.196
You go, Ka times Kb--

00:22:44.196 --> 00:22:45.297
Ka times Kb.

00:22:45.297 --> 00:22:59.678
That's reassuring that Ka times
Kb is equal to H3O+ times OH-.

00:22:59.678 --> 00:23:04.649
And I mean concentrations,
which is Kw.

00:23:04.649 --> 00:23:10.789
That is fun, for the
Bronsted-Lowry acid-base

00:23:10.789 --> 00:23:12.124
conjugate.

00:23:12.124 --> 00:23:15.260
NH4+, NH3, right?

00:23:15.260 --> 00:23:17.295
OK.

00:23:17.295 --> 00:23:20.999
OK, that's Bronsted-Lowry
Lowry acids and bases.

00:23:20.999 --> 00:23:23.101
Here's a little recap.

00:23:23.101 --> 00:23:24.035
Here's a little recap.

00:23:24.035 --> 00:23:26.271
I'm not going to read
through this in detail,

00:23:26.271 --> 00:23:27.572
but I just wanted to give you--

00:23:27.572 --> 00:23:29.141
OK, what have we done so far?

00:23:29.141 --> 00:23:31.076
We've talked about amphoteric.

00:23:31.076 --> 00:23:33.111
We've talked about
conjugate acids and bases.

00:23:33.111 --> 00:23:36.948
These are now written in the
Bronsted-Lowry form, right?

00:23:36.948 --> 00:23:39.518
They accept or give a proton.

00:23:39.518 --> 00:23:43.422
We've talked about the conjugate
acid-base pair, what I just

00:23:43.422 --> 00:23:46.224
drew an example of right
there, neutralization

00:23:46.224 --> 00:23:49.394
reactions, and salt. OK, good.

00:23:49.394 --> 00:23:52.297
These are some concepts
that we have covered that I

00:23:52.297 --> 00:23:54.166
would like you to know about.

00:23:54.166 --> 00:23:56.935
Now but there's a
few more concepts

00:23:56.935 --> 00:23:58.670
that we have to come back to--

00:23:58.670 --> 00:24:00.872
a few more concepts that
we have to come back to.

00:24:00.872 --> 00:24:02.941
And it has to do with
what I started with.

00:24:02.941 --> 00:24:07.078
In the beginning, I said,
you know, these Ks are huge.

00:24:07.078 --> 00:24:10.982
So the equilibrium-- if an
equilibrium constant lies--

00:24:10.982 --> 00:24:15.687
is very large, then you know
you're mostly making product,

00:24:15.687 --> 00:24:16.421
right?

00:24:16.421 --> 00:24:19.424
And so it's essentially
to completion.

00:24:19.424 --> 00:24:23.328
And I also mentioned
that those are strong,

00:24:23.328 --> 00:24:26.131
strong acids, strong bases--

00:24:26.131 --> 00:24:28.266
strong acids, strong bases.

00:24:28.266 --> 00:24:33.872
Why is an acid or
base strong or weak?

00:24:33.872 --> 00:24:37.609
That's what I want to talk
about next, all right?

00:24:37.609 --> 00:24:39.978
And actually, there are--

00:24:39.978 --> 00:24:44.149
there really aren't that many
strong acids or strong bases.

00:24:44.149 --> 00:24:47.686
In fact, they're mostly here.

00:24:47.686 --> 00:24:50.789
These are really, pretty
much most of them.

00:24:50.789 --> 00:24:52.557
Minor [INAUDIBLE] I
don't want to go into.

00:24:52.557 --> 00:24:53.625
But these are really--

00:24:53.625 --> 00:24:56.328
if you think about the general
category of strong acids,

00:24:56.328 --> 00:24:58.930
strong bases, this is it.

00:24:58.930 --> 00:25:00.832
Why?

00:25:00.832 --> 00:25:02.801
Why is that?

00:25:02.801 --> 00:25:05.437
Well, it has to do with what
we've been talking about,

00:25:05.437 --> 00:25:06.037
all right?

00:25:06.037 --> 00:25:07.806
So those are strong acids.

00:25:07.806 --> 00:25:08.740
Those are strong bases.

00:25:11.309 --> 00:25:15.013
It has to do with dissociation.

00:25:15.013 --> 00:25:17.215
It has to do with dissociation.

00:25:17.215 --> 00:25:25.156
So if I take HCl and
I put it in water,

00:25:25.156 --> 00:25:28.093
it almost completely
dissociates.

00:25:28.093 --> 00:25:31.096
That's why it's a strong acid.

00:25:31.096 --> 00:25:35.500
Now there's confusion
around this.

00:25:35.500 --> 00:25:36.635
And I want to make that--

00:25:36.635 --> 00:25:37.936
I want to make it very clear.

00:25:37.936 --> 00:25:39.704
There is a big distinction
I want to make.

00:25:44.042 --> 00:25:47.712
Because if something
is a weak acid,

00:25:47.712 --> 00:25:51.883
meaning it's not one of
these, what that means is it

00:25:51.883 --> 00:25:54.219
doesn't dissociate very much.

00:25:54.219 --> 00:25:55.153
That's what it means.

00:25:55.153 --> 00:25:58.924
It doesn't have anything to do
with concentration, all right?

00:25:58.924 --> 00:26:00.492
So let's take a weak acid.

00:26:00.492 --> 00:26:02.160
Let's take vinegar, all right?

00:26:02.160 --> 00:26:04.696
So if I take a weak acid--

00:26:07.332 --> 00:26:08.833
we see vinegar as CH3COOH.

00:26:13.338 --> 00:26:15.540
You know what's nice about
writing them out this way?

00:26:15.540 --> 00:26:17.342
You say, why didn't
you put the H in there,

00:26:17.342 --> 00:26:22.480
and make it CH4, and CO2,
or put the two Cs together?

00:26:22.480 --> 00:26:24.683
Oftentimes you'll see
molecules written out this way,

00:26:24.683 --> 00:26:26.952
because this tells
us a little bit

00:26:26.952 --> 00:26:28.620
of a hint about connectivity.

00:26:28.620 --> 00:26:31.556
And for acids and bases,
it's actually useful.

00:26:31.556 --> 00:26:33.224
Because you see the H there?

00:26:33.224 --> 00:26:34.993
That's the one that's
going to trade.

00:26:34.993 --> 00:26:38.363
That's the Bronsted-Lowry
transfer H, all right?

00:26:38.363 --> 00:26:39.998
So often, for acids
and bases, you'll

00:26:39.998 --> 00:26:42.601
see that written
out in this way.

00:26:42.601 --> 00:26:44.869
Makes it easier to think about.

00:26:44.869 --> 00:26:50.542
And if I mix this into
water, then what do I get?

00:26:50.542 --> 00:26:52.410
Well, I'm going to get H3O--

00:26:52.410 --> 00:26:54.012
I'm going to write
this down here--

00:26:54.012 --> 00:26:57.916
H3O+-- that's an acid, right--

00:26:57.916 --> 00:27:00.251
plus CH3COO-.

00:27:03.989 --> 00:27:06.691
There's the H that came off--

00:27:06.691 --> 00:27:08.093
proton transfer.

00:27:08.093 --> 00:27:12.397
So why is it weak?

00:27:12.397 --> 00:27:18.169
Well, it's weak because here,
the acid equilibrium constant

00:27:18.169 --> 00:27:22.574
is something like 10
to the minus fifth.

00:27:22.574 --> 00:27:30.582
So even if I have a lot, you do
even if I had like 1 mole per

00:27:30.582 --> 00:27:35.220
liter of this stuff in
solution, it wouldn't--

00:27:35.220 --> 00:27:36.888
most of it wouldn't dissolve.

00:27:36.888 --> 00:27:39.791
In fact, much less than a
percent of vinegar actually

00:27:39.791 --> 00:27:44.195
gives me H3O+, all right?

00:27:44.195 --> 00:27:47.332
But you think, oh,
just add more vinegar.

00:27:47.332 --> 00:27:48.500
No.

00:27:48.500 --> 00:27:53.238
No, because we're talking
about concentrations, right?

00:27:53.238 --> 00:27:56.374
It's the concentration
in that water.

00:27:56.374 --> 00:27:58.143
I'm locked into that by this.

00:27:58.143 --> 00:27:59.678
This is an equilibrium constant.

00:27:59.678 --> 00:28:01.346
And it's talking about
the concentration

00:28:01.346 --> 00:28:02.781
that I get in the water.

00:28:05.283 --> 00:28:06.284
I'm stuck.

00:28:06.284 --> 00:28:10.755
I can't be-- that's how
much you dissociate, right?

00:28:10.755 --> 00:28:13.458
That's how much you
associate as a concentration.

00:28:16.027 --> 00:28:19.264
So it's more acidic than
water for sure, all right?

00:28:19.264 --> 00:28:22.300
It's 100 times more acidic
than water, all right,

00:28:22.300 --> 00:28:24.335
10 to the minus fifth.

00:28:24.335 --> 00:28:27.439
But it's never going to be a
strong acid, because I don't

00:28:27.439 --> 00:28:30.075
give very much of the protons.

00:28:30.075 --> 00:28:32.577
So this is the key that
I want to make clear.

00:28:32.577 --> 00:28:34.646
This is the key, OK?

00:28:34.646 --> 00:28:37.315
It's very important.

00:28:37.315 --> 00:28:41.486
It gets its own board.

00:28:41.486 --> 00:28:44.489
The strength of an acid, OK?

00:28:44.489 --> 00:28:59.971
So acid concentration is
not equal to acid strength.

00:29:03.742 --> 00:29:05.310
More, and more, and
more vinegar-- no.

00:29:07.846 --> 00:29:10.849
It doesn't matter, because
it's about association.

00:29:10.849 --> 00:29:13.785
See, this is about--

00:29:13.785 --> 00:29:18.123
this is a function
of the solubility.

00:29:18.123 --> 00:29:20.291
So how much can I get in there?

00:29:20.291 --> 00:29:22.694
We talked about all
that a lot already.

00:29:22.694 --> 00:29:27.265
That's a different thing,
solubility, the Ksp.

00:29:27.265 --> 00:29:28.600
How much of this can I dissolve?

00:29:28.600 --> 00:29:30.101
Great.

00:29:30.101 --> 00:29:35.340
I can dissolve a lot
of vinegar in water.

00:29:35.340 --> 00:29:37.408
And it doesn't crash out, right?

00:29:37.408 --> 00:29:38.476
That's wonderful.

00:29:38.476 --> 00:29:41.379
Doesn't make it a strong acid.

00:29:41.379 --> 00:29:53.458
This is about
dissociation, all right?

00:29:53.458 --> 00:30:00.999
And so if you think about what
makes an acid a strong acid--

00:30:00.999 --> 00:30:03.935
or for that-- or a weak acid,
or what the relative strengths

00:30:03.935 --> 00:30:06.871
of acids are-- and the
same holds for bases,

00:30:06.871 --> 00:30:08.139
just the other way around--

00:30:08.139 --> 00:30:15.246
it has to do with how easy it
is to take that H off or add it

00:30:15.246 --> 00:30:16.848
on, right?

00:30:16.848 --> 00:30:19.884
That's what it has to do with.

00:30:19.884 --> 00:30:22.320
And so if you look
at-- for example,

00:30:22.320 --> 00:30:29.194
if you look at just a
little series, all right--

00:30:29.194 --> 00:30:34.032
if you look at a series
there, even the strong acids

00:30:34.032 --> 00:30:36.968
have differences.

00:30:36.968 --> 00:30:37.769
They're all strong.

00:30:37.769 --> 00:30:39.971
They go mostly to completion.

00:30:39.971 --> 00:30:42.040
But if you think about it
in terms of what we just

00:30:42.040 --> 00:30:49.848
talked about, the
acid strength is

00:30:49.848 --> 00:30:57.622
going to go roughly
often opposite trend

00:30:57.622 --> 00:30:59.090
as bond strength.

00:31:05.263 --> 00:31:13.238
That's of the proton
transfer of the H, all right?

00:31:13.238 --> 00:31:14.706
There is an H--

00:31:14.706 --> 00:31:17.375
HCl, HBr, HI.

00:31:17.375 --> 00:31:22.146
Which will be the strongest
acid of those three, right?

00:31:22.146 --> 00:31:23.848
Well, we now know.

00:31:23.848 --> 00:31:35.126
This must be the
strongest, right?

00:31:35.126 --> 00:31:39.530
Opposite-- because this
is going to be easier

00:31:39.530 --> 00:31:41.633
to get the hydrogens
off in solution.

00:31:44.335 --> 00:31:48.439
And you can think about the weak
acids in the same exact way.

00:31:48.439 --> 00:31:49.941
These are the strong acids.

00:31:49.941 --> 00:31:51.442
This holds for all--

00:31:51.442 --> 00:31:53.244
you know, it's a
proton transfer.

00:31:53.244 --> 00:31:56.748
It's all about the
strength of the proton.

00:31:56.748 --> 00:31:59.751
Neutralization is
a proton transfer.

00:31:59.751 --> 00:32:02.053
Whether you make an acid
strong or how strong it is

00:32:02.053 --> 00:32:04.322
depends on how many of those
protons go into solution.

00:32:06.991 --> 00:32:08.793
And so you can look
up charts like this.

00:32:08.793 --> 00:32:10.295
And I love looking
at this stuff,

00:32:10.295 --> 00:32:12.764
because it makes you think
about the chemistry, all right?

00:32:12.764 --> 00:32:13.731
So here you go.

00:32:13.731 --> 00:32:19.103
You've got, OK, acid, different
acids, molecular formula,

00:32:19.103 --> 00:32:21.205
all right, structural
formula, conjugate--

00:32:21.205 --> 00:32:24.609
notice they're putting
that H there in blue.

00:32:24.609 --> 00:32:26.544
Oh, that's so helpful.

00:32:26.544 --> 00:32:29.147
And you just look at the blue
H, and you think about it

00:32:29.147 --> 00:32:32.417
as dissociating, just like
Bronsten and Lowry did.

00:32:32.417 --> 00:32:34.085
You think about
that as coming off.

00:32:34.085 --> 00:32:37.622
And then you think about the
conjugate base, all right,

00:32:37.622 --> 00:32:38.957
that's formed on the other side.

00:32:38.957 --> 00:32:40.358
And then you go
over here, and you

00:32:40.358 --> 00:32:44.996
think, oh, this tells me how
much it's coming up, how often.

00:32:44.996 --> 00:32:49.300
This tells me the equilibrium
of putting this into solution.

00:32:49.300 --> 00:32:50.301
You say, this is--

00:32:50.301 --> 00:32:53.538
which one of these is going
to be weaker or stronger,

00:32:53.538 --> 00:32:55.106
all right?

00:32:55.106 --> 00:32:59.544
Well, and what does that mean
about the bond strength itself?

00:32:59.544 --> 00:33:01.846
What does that mean about
how easy it is to dissociate?

00:33:01.846 --> 00:33:02.647
We now know.

00:33:02.647 --> 00:33:04.983
And we can think about
that, and understand that.

00:33:04.983 --> 00:33:09.253
These are not-- these
may look similar,

00:33:09.253 --> 00:33:12.991
these Hs attached to carbon or
oxygen. What's the difference?

00:33:12.991 --> 00:33:16.294
Big difference when
you put them in water.

00:33:16.294 --> 00:33:18.596
A lot more or a lot
less might come off.

00:33:18.596 --> 00:33:21.899
Changing the acid strength--
the more that comes off,

00:33:21.899 --> 00:33:27.572
the more the dissociation
is to the right, all right?

00:33:27.572 --> 00:33:31.042
Oh, and the lower,
right, the higher

00:33:31.042 --> 00:33:32.810
this number, because
the equilibrium moves

00:33:32.810 --> 00:33:34.846
over to the right, all right?

00:33:34.846 --> 00:33:38.783
And the less that comes off, the
lower this equilibrium constant

00:33:38.783 --> 00:33:40.885
is, Ka, right--

00:33:40.885 --> 00:33:42.353
the lower it is.

00:33:42.353 --> 00:33:43.721
The higher the pK--

00:33:43.721 --> 00:33:46.457
we don't like these
small, tiny numbers.

00:33:46.457 --> 00:33:49.260
You take a minus log,
and you talk about pKs.

00:33:49.260 --> 00:33:51.996
That's fine, all right?

00:33:51.996 --> 00:33:54.332
OK, good.

00:33:54.332 --> 00:33:57.068
This is the kind of
fundamental understanding

00:33:57.068 --> 00:34:00.104
of acids and bases that
I want you guys to have.

00:34:00.104 --> 00:34:03.508
And so if we go back
to neutralization,

00:34:03.508 --> 00:34:06.677
now you say, well, OK,
this makes sense, right?

00:34:06.677 --> 00:34:08.279
We had a strong acid
and a strong base.

00:34:08.279 --> 00:34:10.581
If you had them equivalent,
you'd get a pH of 7.

00:34:10.581 --> 00:34:12.650
Now you know, if you had
a strong acid, weak base,

00:34:12.650 --> 00:34:15.119
weak acid, strong
base, it's going to--

00:34:15.119 --> 00:34:18.422
this is fairly self-explanatory,
less than 7 or greater than 7.

00:34:18.422 --> 00:34:21.525
And now you know also, if it's
a weak acid and a weak base,

00:34:21.525 --> 00:34:23.494
and you're neutralizing
these together,

00:34:23.494 --> 00:34:25.696
it would depend
on the Ka and Kb.

00:34:25.696 --> 00:34:28.599
Because it depends on
how many of those ions

00:34:28.599 --> 00:34:31.101
you put in solution
or can take out.

00:34:31.101 --> 00:34:35.039
And that's given by
the Ka and Kb, OK?

00:34:35.039 --> 00:34:35.840
Good.

00:34:35.840 --> 00:34:36.706
Good.

00:34:36.706 --> 00:34:39.377
So we could answer
questions-- we're now

00:34:39.377 --> 00:34:40.844
armed to answer questions.

00:34:40.844 --> 00:34:42.914
And like I said,
I want to answer--

00:34:42.914 --> 00:34:44.782
we're going to do some
problems on Wednesday.

00:34:44.782 --> 00:34:47.217
We'll talk about the topics
that are going to be on exam 3.

00:34:49.754 --> 00:34:51.355
But we can answer
questions like this.

00:34:51.355 --> 00:34:53.424
Here we are talking
about acids and bases.

00:34:53.424 --> 00:34:56.860
What if I mix a bunch
of them together,

00:34:56.860 --> 00:34:58.162
and they've got different Ka's?

00:34:58.162 --> 00:35:02.033
So think about this, what if
I mix all of these together?

00:35:02.033 --> 00:35:04.969
Can I get my pH, right?

00:35:04.969 --> 00:35:05.870
So here's a question.

00:35:05.870 --> 00:35:10.241
You have 1 mole per liter
of HCN with that acid

00:35:10.241 --> 00:35:13.478
dissociation constant and
5 moles per liter of HNO2

00:35:13.478 --> 00:35:14.645
with that one.

00:35:14.645 --> 00:35:18.049
What's-- OK, you write
that dissociation reactions

00:35:18.049 --> 00:35:19.684
and calculate the
pH of the solution.

00:35:22.520 --> 00:35:24.288
That's a good question, right?

00:35:24.288 --> 00:35:25.189
So let's see.

00:35:25.189 --> 00:35:27.191
Now how would I answer it?

00:35:27.191 --> 00:35:31.829
Well, oh, Bronsted-Lowry,
don't want to erase you,

00:35:31.829 --> 00:35:34.165
but I have to.

00:35:34.165 --> 00:35:35.299
I have no choice.

00:35:38.102 --> 00:35:39.337
So let's answer that question.

00:35:41.973 --> 00:35:43.841
The way that we
think about this,

00:35:43.841 --> 00:35:45.576
it's telling us what to do.

00:35:45.576 --> 00:35:48.880
Write the dissociation
reactions for each species.

00:35:48.880 --> 00:35:50.515
OK, so I'm going
to write it down.

00:35:50.515 --> 00:35:51.482
So I have HCN.

00:35:51.482 --> 00:36:01.826
HCN, right, is going
to go to H+ plus CN-.

00:36:01.826 --> 00:36:06.430
Now Ka, for this,
is equal to H+--

00:36:06.430 --> 00:36:09.567
oh boy, did I drop
my subscripts-- times

00:36:09.567 --> 00:36:18.609
CN- divided by HCN
concentrations.

00:36:18.609 --> 00:36:22.146
But we're back to solubility
product days, right?

00:36:22.146 --> 00:36:24.081
This is an equilibrium.

00:36:24.081 --> 00:36:26.217
And so it's going both ways.

00:36:26.217 --> 00:36:29.287
But you know from the
coefficients here--

00:36:29.287 --> 00:36:31.389
all right, you know
from those coefficients

00:36:31.389 --> 00:36:35.760
that if I dissociate x amount
of this, then that's how much

00:36:35.760 --> 00:36:40.198
of this and this that
I make, all right?

00:36:40.198 --> 00:36:43.234
So now I know, right, that--

00:36:43.234 --> 00:36:47.038
well, OK-- Ka times--

00:36:47.038 --> 00:36:48.439
if I just bring
this over there--

00:36:48.439 --> 00:36:54.045
HCN is equal to x
squared, where x--

00:36:54.045 --> 00:36:57.548
let's let x equal
concentration of H+,

00:36:57.548 --> 00:37:00.952
which equals the
concentration of CN-.

00:37:00.952 --> 00:37:05.723
Been there, done that, right,
from the solubility product

00:37:05.723 --> 00:37:08.226
problems that we've done.

00:37:08.226 --> 00:37:12.830
And so you can get, from this,
that the concentration of H+,

00:37:12.830 --> 00:37:21.806
which is also H3O+, is equal
to 2.5 times 10 to the minus 5

00:37:21.806 --> 00:37:23.107
moles per liter.

00:37:23.107 --> 00:37:23.841
Good.

00:37:23.841 --> 00:37:24.342
OK.

00:37:24.342 --> 00:37:26.310
But you can keep going.

00:37:26.310 --> 00:37:31.949
And I'm not going
to do all the math.

00:37:31.949 --> 00:37:39.023
But you could go through
the same exercise with HNO2.

00:37:39.023 --> 00:37:44.462
So for HNO2, you get
that the concentration

00:37:44.462 --> 00:37:47.498
of these ions that you
get from that dissociation

00:37:47.498 --> 00:37:53.804
is around 2 times 10 to the
minus 3 moles per liter.

00:37:53.804 --> 00:37:58.709
And by the way, maybe
I got less than 10

00:37:58.709 --> 00:38:02.013
to the minus seventh
out of these things--

00:38:02.013 --> 00:38:02.813
maybe.

00:38:02.813 --> 00:38:07.585
Maybe if I did, then,
if I did, then I'd

00:38:07.585 --> 00:38:12.156
also need to take into
account pure water, right?

00:38:12.156 --> 00:38:18.863
So from pure water, we know, if
you just had water by itself,

00:38:18.863 --> 00:38:21.399
then you'd have,
the concentration

00:38:21.399 --> 00:38:28.773
here is 10 to the minus
seventh moles per liter.

00:38:28.773 --> 00:38:30.574
But now you just look
at this, and you say,

00:38:30.574 --> 00:38:31.475
OK, I got my answer.

00:38:31.475 --> 00:38:36.047
Because everything's a lot
lower than this one thing.

00:38:36.047 --> 00:38:38.482
So this is going to dominate.

00:38:38.482 --> 00:38:39.417
I mix it all together.

00:38:39.417 --> 00:38:41.152
But you see, it doesn't matter.

00:38:41.152 --> 00:38:45.756
The other things aren't able to
disassociate nearly as many H+

00:38:45.756 --> 00:38:46.324
ions--

00:38:46.324 --> 00:38:47.458
they're just not able to--

00:38:47.458 --> 00:38:50.294
as HNO2.

00:38:50.294 --> 00:38:52.129
So this dominates.

00:38:52.129 --> 00:38:53.964
And you can just calculate
the pH from this.

00:38:53.964 --> 00:38:56.400
So you're going to get,
like, two, three digits down

00:38:56.400 --> 00:38:56.967
some effect.

00:38:56.967 --> 00:38:58.836
But it's OK, right?

00:38:58.836 --> 00:39:00.871
From this, you can get the pH.

00:39:00.871 --> 00:39:10.648
And the pH is equal to 1.35.

00:39:10.648 --> 00:39:15.052
The total contribution of
H+ ions in that solution is

00:39:15.052 --> 00:39:19.690
roughly given by this
contribution here, OK?

00:39:19.690 --> 00:39:23.260
Might not have been,
but it was in that case.

00:39:23.260 --> 00:39:25.930
OK, how about this one?

00:39:25.930 --> 00:39:31.302
So here's another-- OK, so we
could take another example.

00:39:31.302 --> 00:39:34.605
Let's stay over here.

00:39:34.605 --> 00:39:38.042
OK, so in this example,
I'm barely giving you

00:39:38.042 --> 00:39:40.077
anything here, it seems like.

00:39:40.077 --> 00:39:41.946
But I give you enough.

00:39:41.946 --> 00:39:45.816
I've got a 0.2 moles per
liter solution of a weak acid.

00:39:45.816 --> 00:39:48.986
And it's 9.4% dissociated.

00:39:48.986 --> 00:39:51.522
That's the key.

00:39:51.522 --> 00:39:52.123
That's the key.

00:39:52.123 --> 00:39:55.393
Because remember, this
is-- now we've learned

00:39:55.393 --> 00:39:56.627
the Bronsted-Lowry definition.

00:39:56.627 --> 00:40:01.399
It's all about H+
in or coming out.

00:40:01.399 --> 00:40:03.868
That's what-- there it is.

00:40:03.868 --> 00:40:06.804
Now I erase it though.

00:40:06.804 --> 00:40:07.938
It's all about the H+.

00:40:07.938 --> 00:40:13.177
So if I know 9.4%
dissociated, I know something

00:40:13.177 --> 00:40:16.881
about what kind of
acid that is, right?

00:40:16.881 --> 00:40:25.423
So if I have, now, HA plus
H2O, and that goes to H3O+--

00:40:25.423 --> 00:40:32.863
I've switched back-- plus A-,
then how much dissociated?

00:40:32.863 --> 00:40:38.536
Well, 0.2 moles per
liter times 0.094, right?

00:40:38.536 --> 00:40:43.374
So 0.094, that's the percent--

00:40:43.374 --> 00:40:43.941
is that right?

00:40:43.941 --> 00:40:49.647
Yes, 9.4%-- times
0.2 moles per liter.

00:40:49.647 --> 00:40:57.788
And that equals
0.019-ish, right?

00:40:57.788 --> 00:41:00.724
That's how much HA--

00:41:00.724 --> 00:41:03.594
HA-- dissociated.

00:41:07.665 --> 00:41:09.767
But if I know that,
and I know my reaction,

00:41:09.767 --> 00:41:12.837
and I got the stoichiometric
coefficients from the reaction,

00:41:12.837 --> 00:41:18.342
then I also know how
much H3O+ and A- formed.

00:41:18.342 --> 00:41:24.081
If that much dissociated,
then that much formed, right?

00:41:24.081 --> 00:41:25.483
Because they're-- right?

00:41:25.483 --> 00:41:26.417
That's the reaction.

00:41:26.417 --> 00:41:27.985
Those are the coefficients.

00:41:27.985 --> 00:41:29.386
And so I can get it all.

00:41:29.386 --> 00:41:35.359
And I can calculate Ka, which
equals 2 times 10 to the minus

00:41:35.359 --> 00:41:37.828
third.

00:41:37.828 --> 00:41:39.630
I didn't give the pH.

00:41:39.630 --> 00:41:42.766
All I gave you was how
much of it dissociated.

00:41:42.766 --> 00:41:44.435
I didn't even tell
you what acid it was.

00:41:44.435 --> 00:41:49.440
But that's OK, because we now
know that this is what counts.

00:41:49.440 --> 00:41:53.577
This dissociation, how
many of these Hs per liter,

00:41:53.577 --> 00:41:57.147
how many of those go into
solution is what matters.

00:41:57.147 --> 00:41:59.717
OK, now there's another thing
that I want to talk about.

00:41:59.717 --> 00:42:00.885
And we'll do some more--

00:42:00.885 --> 00:42:03.187
you know, a little
bit more, like I

00:42:03.187 --> 00:42:05.189
said, problems on Wednesday.

00:42:05.189 --> 00:42:07.525
There's just one more thing
I want you to know about

00:42:07.525 --> 00:42:09.860
related to acids
and bases, and that

00:42:09.860 --> 00:42:13.898
is that you can
have more than one.

00:42:13.898 --> 00:42:17.501
All these examples, if
you go back to this list,

00:42:17.501 --> 00:42:22.406
those all had one hydrogen
that was ever going to leave,

00:42:22.406 --> 00:42:23.207
let's say.

00:42:23.207 --> 00:42:24.975
I mean, OK, ever--
maybe if you went up

00:42:24.975 --> 00:42:27.278
to the middle of the sun or
something, you'd get those.

00:42:27.278 --> 00:42:29.446
But this is-- you know,
in normal conditions,

00:42:29.446 --> 00:42:31.782
these are the ones that are
going to leave, and no more.

00:42:31.782 --> 00:42:36.020
But you can have molecules
like phosphoric acid where--

00:42:36.020 --> 00:42:38.055
look at all those protons.

00:42:38.055 --> 00:42:39.890
So here's acetic acid.

00:42:39.890 --> 00:42:41.525
That's the one that's
willing to leave.

00:42:41.525 --> 00:42:42.126
Those are not.

00:42:42.126 --> 00:42:43.327
They're on the carbon.

00:42:43.327 --> 00:42:44.728
Way too hard to get them off.

00:42:44.728 --> 00:42:48.666
This one-- OK, in solution,
sure, to a certain extent.

00:42:48.666 --> 00:42:52.703
In phosphoric acid, I've
got three hydrogens.

00:42:52.703 --> 00:42:55.472
And so that's called
a multiprotic acid.

00:42:55.472 --> 00:42:56.106
Why?

00:42:56.106 --> 00:42:58.642
Because protic-- proton--

00:42:58.642 --> 00:43:01.812
how many protons, right?

00:43:01.812 --> 00:43:05.316
It could be monoprotic, like
the ones we've talked about,

00:43:05.316 --> 00:43:09.553
or it could be multiprotic,
like phosphoric acid.

00:43:09.553 --> 00:43:12.222
And so if you think
about this the way

00:43:12.222 --> 00:43:16.226
we now think about
acids and bases,

00:43:16.226 --> 00:43:19.597
we think about an acid
as a dissociation, right,

00:43:19.597 --> 00:43:22.366
with some dissociation
equilibrium constant.

00:43:25.970 --> 00:43:28.639
And so if you think about
phosphoric acid, H3PO4--

00:43:31.375 --> 00:43:35.579
oh, let's put the
water in, H2O, liquid.

00:43:38.082 --> 00:43:45.789
And that goes to
H2PO4- plus H3O+.

00:43:45.789 --> 00:43:49.293
Well the Ka for this one is--

00:43:49.293 --> 00:43:52.630
so if I put the Ka's
in here, this one

00:43:52.630 --> 00:43:58.602
is 7.5 times 10 to the minus 3.

00:43:58.602 --> 00:44:01.005
But see, what happens
is, now, this one

00:44:01.005 --> 00:44:02.973
has two more hydrogens
that could come off.

00:44:02.973 --> 00:44:05.609
So you could write those
dissociation reactions as well.

00:44:05.609 --> 00:44:13.651
So H2PO4-, if you put that
in solution, which it is,

00:44:13.651 --> 00:44:19.690
then that could give
you HPO4 2-, all right?

00:44:19.690 --> 00:44:24.628
H2, H-- it's a proton transfer.

00:44:24.628 --> 00:44:28.298
Minus, 2-, all right--

00:44:28.298 --> 00:44:29.166
plus H3O+.

00:44:31.935 --> 00:44:38.142
And there's one
more, plus one more.

00:44:38.142 --> 00:44:41.545
This dissociation constant
is much, much smaller,

00:44:41.545 --> 00:44:46.817
6.2 times 10 to the minus 8.

00:44:46.817 --> 00:44:54.291
And the third one is 4.2
times 10 to the minus 13th.

00:44:54.291 --> 00:44:56.727
So they're smaller,
but they happen.

00:44:56.727 --> 00:44:58.228
And that's important.

00:44:58.228 --> 00:44:59.463
And it can be very important.

00:44:59.463 --> 00:45:00.831
It depends on the molecule.

00:45:00.831 --> 00:45:04.968
But in this case, it's not just
the parent, that acid there

00:45:04.968 --> 00:45:05.936
that's losing a proton.

00:45:05.936 --> 00:45:09.006
But then, this one can
also lose a proton.

00:45:09.006 --> 00:45:11.308
It can also be an acid.

00:45:11.308 --> 00:45:14.712
It just has an equilibrium
that's not as far over.

00:45:14.712 --> 00:45:19.883
So it's not going to give as
many H+ into solution, OK?

00:45:19.883 --> 00:45:21.685
It's important to know about.

00:45:21.685 --> 00:45:24.154
Then finally, the last
thing I want you to know,

00:45:24.154 --> 00:45:27.191
you will not be tested on
this, but it's important.

00:45:27.191 --> 00:45:29.626
Because here's where we are.

00:45:29.626 --> 00:45:30.627
Here's where we are.

00:45:30.627 --> 00:45:35.999
Arrhenius said, right
before Thanksgiving,

00:45:35.999 --> 00:45:39.636
it's all about the
H+ and the OH-.

00:45:39.636 --> 00:45:43.073
That got us really far, got
us through Thanksgiving.

00:45:43.073 --> 00:45:45.943
But then today, we're like, no,
it's got to be more general.

00:45:45.943 --> 00:45:48.812
It's just proton transfer.

00:45:48.812 --> 00:45:52.349
No, don't tell me about OH-.

00:45:52.349 --> 00:45:54.852
It's just proton transfer.

00:45:54.852 --> 00:45:58.122
And so then comes Lewis.

00:45:58.122 --> 00:45:59.256
Oh, we know Lewis.

00:45:59.256 --> 00:46:00.991
We've already talked
a lot about Lewis.

00:46:00.991 --> 00:46:05.529
Lewis said, why are you guys
all obsessed about protons?

00:46:05.529 --> 00:46:09.433
And he said it in just that way.

00:46:09.433 --> 00:46:11.869
An acid is much more
general even than that.

00:46:11.869 --> 00:46:14.905
An acid is any
species, anything that

00:46:14.905 --> 00:46:16.607
except a pair of electrons.

00:46:16.607 --> 00:46:20.177
Remember, Lewis was all about
those electrons and the octets.

00:46:20.177 --> 00:46:24.848
And a base is any species that
can donate a pair of electrons.

00:46:24.848 --> 00:46:29.419
So Lewis correctly took
a much more general view

00:46:29.419 --> 00:46:31.155
and said, no, acids
and bases are not

00:46:31.155 --> 00:46:32.723
even about proton transfer.

00:46:32.723 --> 00:46:36.326
They're about electron transfer.

00:46:36.326 --> 00:46:39.062
And again, I'm not going to test
you on Lewis, acids, and bases,

00:46:39.062 --> 00:46:40.864
but I wanted you to
have that full picture.

00:46:40.864 --> 00:46:44.134
That means, by the
way, aluminum 3+--

00:46:44.134 --> 00:46:46.537
take an aluminum
atom, make it 3+.

00:46:46.537 --> 00:46:49.540
It wants electrons.

00:46:49.540 --> 00:46:51.675
It's an acid.

00:46:51.675 --> 00:46:54.645
It's an acid, all right?

00:46:54.645 --> 00:46:56.880
Doesn't have a prot-- it
wants electrons though.

00:46:56.880 --> 00:46:58.982
That makes it an acid
according to Lewis.

00:46:58.982 --> 00:47:02.986
So that concludes
acids and bases.

00:47:02.986 --> 00:47:06.056
And I'll see you
guys on Wednesday.

